<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout>
    <title>Section 1.5 &amp; 1.6: Quadratic Functions</title>

    <alert>
        MTH 124
    </alert>

    <p workspace="0.15in">
        A <em>quadratic function</em> is one that may be written in the form
        <md>
            q(x)=ax^2 + bx + c
        </md>
        where <m>a \ne 0</m>.
        That is, there is an <m>x^2</m> term but no higher powers of <m>x</m>.
    </p>

    <activity>
        <title>Activity 1.5.2</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                Open a browser and go to Desmos, <term>desmos.com</term>.
                In a cell in Desmos, enter <m>q(x) = ax^2 + bx + c</m> in
                exactly that form; you will be prompted to add sliders for
                <m>a, b,</m> and <m>c</m>.
                Do so.
                Then begin exploring with the sliders and respond to the
                following questions.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Describe how changing the value of <m>a</m> affects the
                    graph of <m>q</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Describe how changing the value of <m>b</m> affects the
                    graph of <m>q</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Describe how changing the value of <m>c</m> affects the
                    graph of <m>q</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Which parameter seems to have the simplest effect?
                    Which parameter seems to have the most complicated effect?
                    Why?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Is it possible to find a formula for a quadratic function
                    that passes through the points <m>(0,8), (1,12), (2,12)</m>?
                    If yes, do so; if not, explain why not.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </activity>

    <assemblage>
        <title>Quadratic Formula - Solutions to <m>q(x)=0</m></title>

        <p>
            Let <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m> be real numbers with
            <m>a\ne0</m>.
            The equation <m>ax^2+bx+c =0</m> can have <m>0, 1</m>, or <m>2</m>
            real solutions.
            These real solutions are given by the
            <term>quadratic formula</term>,
            <md>
                x = \frac{-b \pm \sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a}
            </md>
            provided <m>b^2 - 4ac \ge 0</m>.
        </p>
    </assemblage>

    <activity>
        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the solutions to <m>3x^2-18x-21=0</m> using the
                    quadratic formula from the last page.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </activity>

    <assemblage>
        <title>The vertex of a parabola</title>

        <p>
            The quadratic function <m>y=q(x) = ax^2+bx+c</m> has its vertex at
            the <m>x-</m> coordinate <m>x = -\frac{b}{2a}</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            When <m>a&gt;0</m>, the graph opens upward, and thus the vertex is
            the lowest point on the graph of <m>q</m> (see example on the left).
            When <m>a&lt;0</m>, the graph opens downward, and so the vertex is
            the highest point (see example on the right).
        </p>

        <p>
            The graph is symmetric around the line <m>x = -\frac{b}{2a}</m>.
        </p>
    </assemblage>

    <image source="images/1.5" width="50%"/>
    <assemblage>
        <title>Vertex form of a quadratic function</title>

        <p>
            A quadratic function with vertex <m>(h,k)</m> may be written in the
            form <m>y = a(x-h)^2+k</m>.
            The constant <m>a</m> may be determined from one other function
            value for an input <m>x\ne h</m>.
        </p>
    </assemblage>

    <activity>
        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find an equation for the parabola that has the vertex
                    <m>(1,4)</m> and goes through the point <m>(3,2)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </activity>

    <p>
        Just a note: objects that fall under the force of gravity, like
        baseballs or water balloons or anything, can be modeled by quadratic
        curves.
    </p>

    <p>
        <term>Composite Functions</term>
    </p>

    <definition>
        <title>Definition 1.6.2</title>

        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> are functions such that
                <m>g:A\rightarrow B</m> and <m>f:B\rightarrow C</m>, we define
                the <term>composition of <m>f</m> and <m>g</m></term> to be the
                new function <m>h:A\rightarrow C</m> given by
                <md>
                    h(t) = f(g(t)).
                </md>
                We also sometimes use the notation <m>h = f\circ g</m>, where
                <m>f\circ g</m> is the single function defined by
                <m>(f\circ g)(t) = f(g(t))</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <activity>
        <title>Activity 1.6.2</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                Let functions <m>p</m> and <m>q</m> be given by the graphs below
                (which are each piecewise linear - that is, parts that look like
                straight lines are straight lines) and let <m>f</m> and <m>g</m>
                be given by the table below.
            </p>

            <image source="images/1.6.2g" width="50%"/>
            <image source="images/1.6.2t" width="50%"/>
            <p>
                Compute each of the following quantities or explain why they are
                not defined.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>p(q(0))</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>q(p(0))</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>(p\circ p)(-1)</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>(f\circ g)(2)</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>(g\circ f)(3)</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>g(f(0))</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    For what value(s) of <m>x</m> is <m>f(g(x)) = 4</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    For what value(s) of <m>x</m> is <m>q(p(x)) = 1</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </activity>

    <activity>
        <title>Activity 1.6.3. Context</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                Let <m>F = D(N) = 40+0.25N</m> be Dolbear’s function that
                converts an input of number of chirps per minute to degrees
                Fahrenheit.
            </p>

            <p>
                Let <m>C = G(F) = \frac{5}{9} (F-32)</m> be the function that
                converts an input of degrees Fahrenheit to an output of degrees
                Celsius.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Determine a formula for the new function <m>H = G(D(N))</m>
                    that depends only on the variable <m>N</m>.
                    That is <m>H</m> should equal some function of <m>N</m> and
                    only <m>N</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What is the meaning of the function you found in (a)?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    How does a plot of the new function <m>H = G(D(N))</m>
                    compare to that of Dolbear’s function?
                    Sketch a plot of this new function <m>H</m> on the blank
                    axes to the right of the plot of Dolbear’s function, and
                    discuss the similarities and differences between them.
                    Be sure to label the vertical scale on your axes.
                </p>

                <image source="images/1.6.3c" width="50%"/>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What is the domain of the function <m>H = G(D(N))</m>?
                    What is its range?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </activity>

    <activity>
        <title>Activity 1.6.4</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                Let <m>f(x) = 2x^2-3x+1</m> and <m>g(x) = \frac{5}{x}</m>.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Compute <m>f(1+h)</m> for an unknown variable <m>h</m> and
                    expand and simplify the result as much as possible by
                    combining like terms.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Use part (a) to determine the most simplified expression you
                    can for the average rate of change of <m>f</m> on the
                    interval <m>[1,1+h]</m>.
                    That is, determine <m>AV_{[1,1+h]}</m> for <m>f</m> and
                    simplify the result as much as possible.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Compute <m>g(1+h)</m>.
                    Is there any valid algebra you can do to write more simply?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Determine the most simplified expression you can for the
                    average rate of change of <m>g</m> on the interval
                    <m>[1,1+h]</m>.
                    That is, determine <m>AV_{[1,1+h]}</m> for <m>g</m> and
                    simplify the result.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </activity>
</handout>